                       · One seed. One demo. Forever. ·

 ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
                       SEEDSCENE v0.2  ·  november 2026
          generative demoscene  ·  music + motion from a single seed
 ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                                  THE IDEA                                │
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   Remember typing LOAD"*",8,1 and waiting? Remember the cracktros, the
   copper bars, the sine scrollers, the chiptunes that were better than
   the games they came with? SEEDSCENE brings that back, with a twist:

   type any word -- your name, a date, "1989" -- and it composes a demo
   just for that word. Music and visuals, made on the spot, in your
   browser. Type the same word tomorrow and you get the very same demo,
   down to the last pixel. Like a game world seed, but for a 90s intro.

   And then it takes you travelling: from the first arcades of the 70s
   and the C64 in 1982, through the Amiga, DOS and VGA, the 32-bit
   consoles and the pocket screens, the 64k intros of the 2000s, to the
   shader art of today.

 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                               WHAT'S INSIDE                              │
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   ▪ seven eras, spans of years, each with its scenes, its music, its
        screens and its sound boards: 1972-1981 the first video games
        1982-1989 home computers · 1990-1993 the demoscene's golden years
        1994-1996 the PC demos · 1997-1999 3D consoles and pocket screens
        2000-2009 the 64k intro · 2010-now shader art
   ▪ THE JOURNEY: a trackmo through the eras, oldest first
   ▪ AUTOPLAY: an endless channel, a new track every 40 seconds
        and the scenes cut on the music -- never the same sequence
        each track mixed into the next, beat on beat, like a DJ set
   ▪ fifty-seven scenes: plasma · tunnel · metaballs · starfield · copper
        fractal · shadebobs · vector balls · dot tunnel · fireworks · fire
        kefrens bars · the bouncing checkered ball · interference rings
        a twister · unlimited bobs · a dual chessboard
        a chessboard floor with its bouncing cube · a rubber cube
        a bump-mapped logo · a cracktro · the card cascade
        the ANSI board · the bouncing lines · the 3D maze · the 3D pipes
        an LCD juggler · a car with a red scanner · footsteps in a cup
        a hopper on a pyramid of cubes · the time car · the moon bike
        water ripple · outrun sunset · raymarched morph
        and the shader art of the 2010s: prism · sculpture · fractal flight
        code rain · the 64k gimbal · the DJ decks
        an anime opening -- and from the arcade: paddles · invaders
        rocks · maze · a road crossing · a vector tube · wireframe tanks
        bricks · light riders · snake
        a robot title fight · a starfighter run · a football match
        a skate park · an artillery duel
        -- the games on a 3D stage that rides the music
   ▪ thirty-six music styles: SID chiptune · italo disco · ST buzz
        synth-pop · arcade attract · cyborg pulse · derelict ship
        new beat · amiga rave · orchestral tracker · dreamwave
        tracker jazz · beast lands · lament · musicdisk ballad
        jolly march · hip-hop blaster · terrace drums
        acid · ambient · gabber · jungle
        eurodance · FM metal · MIDI quest · keygen · trip-hop
        heroic game · star pilot · handheld chip · chip rock and roll
        the anime opening · skate punk · happy hardcore · trance
        synthwave
   ▪ the sound chips of the eras: the C64's SID, the Atari ST's YM2149,
        Paula's samples on the Amiga, the Sound Blaster 16's FM and the
        AWE32's wavetable -- or the synthesizer's own voices
   ▪ sixteen displays: from the black-and-white set, the vector
        monitors and the C64 to the Amiga, VGA, the composite TV, the
        Game Boy and today's screen
   ▪ a real C64 boot screen -- LOAD, the striped border, READY., RUN
   ▪ press A for the about part -- SEEDSCENE's own demo about itself
   ▪ live in the browser, or rendered to an mp4 you can share
   ▪ same seed, same demo: byte for byte, every time
   ▪ no videos, no assets -- only maths (even the samples are its own)

 ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                               HOW TO RUN IT                              │
 └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   npm install
   node bin/seedscene.js live    --seed yourname
   node bin/seedscene.js journey  --seed yourname
   node bin/seedscene.js autoplay --seed yourname --duration 3600

   In live mode press H to see every key. O autoplay · T the journey
   E or 1-7 era
   SPACE scene · UP/DOWN music · S seed · M menu · A about · F full

